title: '[pallet-assets-precompiles] ERC-20 EVM compatibility fixes'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: "## Summary\n\nThree small fixes to the ERC-20 precompile surface so\
    \ it behaves the way mature ERC-20 ecosystem tooling expects.\n\n- **Infallible\
    \ metadata reads.** `name()`, `symbol()`, and `decimals()` previously reverted\
    \ with `\"Metadata not found\"` when an asset had no metadata row.\n\n- **`approve` and\
    \ `permit` saturate on `U256 > Balance::MAX`.** MetaMask, Uniswap, and most DEX\
    \ routers hard-code `type(uint256).max` in approval calls \u2014 the universal\
    \ \"infinite allowance\" idiom. Previously both selectors reverted at the `U256\
    \ \u2192 Balance` conversion because the runtime `Balance` (u128 or u64) can't\
    \ hold `U256::MAX`. Any value exceeding `Balance::MAX` now saturates at `Balance::MAX`\
    \ (lossless: total supply is also capped there, so a larger allowance grants no\
    \ extra spending power). The emitted `Approval` event reports the saturated amount\
    \ so indexers stay in sync with `allowance()`.\n\n- **Removed a\
    \ misleading comment.** The `approve` doc-comment told callers to use `increaseAllowance`\
    \ / `decreaseAllowance` to mitigate the well-known approve front-running race,\
    \ but neither method is exposed by this precompile (and OpenZeppelin dropped them\
    \ in v5.0). Replaced with a pointer to `permit()`, which this precompile *does*\
    \ expose and which actually solves the same problem atomically via signature.\n\
    \n## Test plan\n\n- [x] `cargo test -p pallet-assets-precompiles` passes"
crates:
- name: pallet-assets-precompiles
  bump: patch
